    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ты кто такой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Who are you?' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'you' with 'such'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Что ты улыбаешься?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What are you smiling at?' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'what' with 'are smiling'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Давай, отвечай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Come on, answer.' Notice the word order: 'let's' comes first, and 'answer' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Айнура, это японец?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Aynura, is this a Japanese person?' Chef Viktor links 'Ainura' directly with 'this' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No.' A crisp, everyday conversational reply meaning 'no' that you will hear constantly in Russian dialogue.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он не наш.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He is not ours.' Ainura combines 'he' with 'our' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Слушай, ты перепутал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Listen, you made a mistake.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'listen' with 'mixed up'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Суши делают рядом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They make sushi next door.' Chef Viktor links 'sushi' directly with 'they do' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Шеф, это новый повар.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, this is the new cook.' Notice how 'chef' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'new'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Какой повар?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What cook?' Chef Viktor links 'what a' directly with 'chef' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Он будет делать суши.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He will make sushi.' Senya combines 'he' with 'sushi' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Chef Viktor responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
